WEBVTT

1
00:00:05.267 --> 00:00:10.739
John pulled the diving mask over his face and gave the boat's captain the thumbs-up.

2
00:00:10.740 --> 00:00:17.190
He held on tightly to his underwater camera, leaned back, and let his own weight carry him overboard.

3
00:00:17.191 --> 00:00:25.160
He began to descend to one of the earth's greatest natural wonders: the Great Australian Barrier Reef.

4
00:00:25.161 --> 00:00:29.374
Slowly John's eyes adjusted to the reduced level of light underwater.

5
00:00:29.375 --> 00:00:31.619
He was amazed by what he saw.

6
00:00:31.620 --> 00:00:34.650
Large groups of brightly colored fish swam past.

7
00:00:34.651 --> 00:00:39.437
One group was a brilliant yellow; the next striped with orange and black lines.

8
00:00:39.438 --> 00:00:44.774
Each group was like a wonderful moving painting of amazing colors.

9
00:00:44.775 --> 00:00:52.307
Everywhere he looked, he saw a different kind of marvelous fish, a different expression of nature's beauty.

10
00:00:52.308 --> 00:00:56.510
Then the reef came into view. It looked like an underwater city.

11
00:00:56.511 --> 00:00:59.609
Most of the reef seemed to be covered with green algae.

12
00:00:59.610 --> 00:01:08.647
All around, however, the colorful animals and plants that clung to and swam near the coral reef stood out in sharp contrast.

13
00:01:08.648 --> 00:01:11.854
Blue starfish crawled slowly over the reef.

14
00:01:11.855 --> 00:01:17.619
Strange multicolored fish called flatworms performed an underwater ballet as they swam above it.

15
00:01:17.620 --> 00:01:27.591
Overwhelmed by the beauty of the reef, minutes passed before John remembered that he had his camera; then, quickly, he began to take pictures.

16
00:01:27.592 --> 00:01:34.921
Australia's coral reef, at two thousand kilometers long, is the largest in the world and the one most visited by divers.

17
00:01:34.922 --> 00:01:37.734
But coral reefs are not well-understood.

18
00:01:37.735 --> 00:01:41.570
Many believe coral is a type of plant. It's not.

19
00:01:41.571 --> 00:01:45.249
Corals are small animals that eat the tiniest shellfish.

20
00:01:45.250 --> 00:01:53.537
Most corals swim through the ocean, but others called hard corals make a permanent home for themselves.

21
00:01:53.538 --> 00:01:57.982
Hard corals take carbon and phosphates out of the water to make limestone.

22
00:01:57.983 --> 00:02:02.726
As the limestone structure where they live builds up, it becomes a coral reef.

23
00:02:02.727 --> 00:02:09.501
Each individual coral is known as a coral polyp and can be as small as five millimeters across.

24
00:02:09.502 --> 00:02:15.901
It takes millions of coral polyps, living side by side, to make a coral reef.

25
00:02:15.902 --> 00:02:22.155
Each generation of hard corals builds on the remains of its ancestors, and over time, the reefs grow.

26
00:02:22.156 --> 00:02:28.471
But coral reefs would not be possible without the help of coral algae, a type of plant that lives with corals.

27
00:02:28.472 --> 00:02:35.541
Coral algae produce the phosphates and carbon that the hard corals use to make limestone.

28
00:02:35.542 --> 00:02:39.578
The limestone gives the algae a perfect home, safe from most predators.

29
00:02:39.579 --> 00:02:43.914
In return, the algae help the corals to speed up their production of limestone.

30
00:02:43.915 --> 00:02:54.000
Coral algae, just like any other plant, need sunlight to live, and so coral reefs are almost always fifty meters or less from the surface of the ocean.

31
00:02:54.001 --> 00:02:57.597
The algae and the corals both contribute to building the coral reef.

32
00:02:57.598 --> 00:03:03.866
When two different types of life live together and help one another, we say they have a symbiotic relationship.

33
00:03:03.867 --> 00:03:09.906
Working together, coral polyps and coral algae create a coral reef.

34
00:03:09.907 --> 00:03:17.643
But the coral reef is more than just a collection of corals and algae; thousands of species of animals and plants make coral reefs their home.

35
00:03:17.644 --> 00:03:24.369
Some scientists estimate that over 25 percent of all ocean life lives in or near a coral reef.

36
00:03:24.370 --> 00:03:30.852
Many other animals that live near coral reefs also enjoy symbiotic relationships.

37
00:03:30.853 --> 00:03:35.317
There are anemones that will sting, paralyze, and eat any fish that comes nearby.

38
00:03:35.318 --> 00:03:38.609
But these anemones make an exception for the clown fish.

39
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:45.892
Clown fish help keep the tentacles of the anemones clean, while the anemones provide the clown fish with a safe place to live and breed.

40
00:03:45.893 --> 00:03:55.257
They also share meals. Both animals eat fish, and each enjoys the leftovers that the other creature leaves behind.

41
00:03:55.258 --> 00:03:58.594
The coral reef is also home to both hunter and prey.

42
00:03:58.595 --> 00:04:04.763
The parrot fish can take huge bites out of the coral reef, digesting both corals and algae.

43
00:04:04.764 --> 00:04:11.737
The fish spits out the limestone. Perhaps the oddest predator of the reef is the crown-of-thorns starfish.

44
00:04:11.738 --> 00:04:20.408
It is strange and dangerous. It slowly moves over the coral reef digesting everything in its path.

45
00:04:20.409 --> 00:04:25.468
Unlike most starfish, it has more than 5 limbs (typically between 12 and 20),

46
00:04:25.469 --> 00:04:31.491
and is usually 25 to 30 centimeters wide, but some can grow up to 80 centimeters.

47
00:04:31.492 --> 00:04:37.841
It is bright red and has spines that can inject a poison that would make an unfortunate diver sick for days.

48
00:04:37.842 --> 00:04:40.913
Some fish use the coral reef to hide from predators.

49
00:04:40.914 --> 00:04:44.913
But predators hide inside the coral reef as well.

50
00:04:44.914 --> 00:04:50.019
The moray eel will suddenly leap out from its hiding place to eat a passing fish.

51
00:04:50.020 --> 00:04:52.571
The octopus, too, can hide in the reef.

52
00:04:52.572 --> 00:04:57.168
It changes color like a chameleon, so that it looks like the surrounding coral reef.

53
00:04:57.169 --> 00:05:02.267
It can squeeze itself into almost any opening and wait for prey to approach.

54
00:05:02.268 --> 00:05:09.904
A crab might be crawling near the reef, only to be captured by the octopus' eight tentacles and quickly eaten.

55
00:05:09.905 --> 00:05:19.157
After taking many photographs, John surfaced. Every photograph of a coral reef is valuable, for there may come a time when coral reefs are extinct.

56
00:05:19.158 --> 00:05:28.723
Coral polyps and coral algae are sensitive to temperature, and global warming is heating the ocean, making it harder for reefs to grow.

57
00:05:28.724 --> 00:05:32.250
Some divers chip off pieces of the reefs to take home.

58
00:05:32.251 --> 00:05:38.513
Some people poison the water near the reefs in order to collect the beautiful fish and sell them for aquariums.

59
00:05:38.514 --> 00:05:42.711
The poison only paralyzes the fish, but it kills the corals.

60
00:05:42.712 --> 00:05:46.821
Even worse, some fishermen use dynamite near coral reefs.

61
00:05:46.822 --> 00:05:52.820
The fish are killed and float to the surface, while huge parts of the coral reef are damaged.

62
00:05:52.821 --> 00:05:57.751
Many coral reefs are shrinking; they are being damaged faster than they can grow.

63
00:05:57.752 --> 00:06:02.553
John hopes that more people learn to appreciate the beauty and importance of coral reefs.

64
00:06:02.554 --> 00:06:06.232
Perhaps then, he thinks, more people will work to save the reefs,

65
00:06:06.233 --> 00:06:14.385
and future generations will always be able to visit the marvelous underwater world of the ocean's coral reefs.
